WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.417
INTERVAL represents duration.

00:00:02.417 --> 00:00:08.602
You can add an interval to a date or timestamp to create a future or past point.

00:00:08.602 --> 00:00:12.997
Timestamp subtraction also returns an interval.

00:00:12.997 --> 00:00:20.862
Intervals are central to PostgreSQL temporal arithmetic and replace many dialect-specific date-add patterns.

00:00:20.862 --> 00:00:24.042
Choose temporal types based on meaning.

00:00:24.042 --> 00:00:27.752
DATE is for calendar dates without time of day.

00:00:27.752 --> 00:00:32.817
TIMESTAMP stores a date and time without a time-zone interpretation.

00:00:32.817 --> 00:00:39.617
TIMESTAMPTZ represents an instant in time and is usually safer for events that cross regions or

00:00:39.617 --> 00:00:41.474
daylight-saving changes.
